<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-4-polar-area" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Polar Areas and Lengths</title>

  <p><alert>Area in polar coordinates.</alert> Assume <m>r=f(\theta)\geq 0</m> for <m>\theta\in[a,b]</m> to avoid complications with signs, and consider the region inside the curve, defined by <m>0\leq r\leq f(\theta)</m> for <m>\theta\in[a,b]</m>.
  Apply Slice Analysis (<m>\S5.2</m>), splitting the area <m>A</m> into
  <m>n</m> thin wedges <m>\Delta A_i</m> over <m>[\theta_i,\theta_{i+1}]</m>:</p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-10-4-delta-a-placeholder">
    <caption>Placeholder for 10-4-Delta-A.</caption>
    <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 10-4-Delta-A.png.</shortdescription>
      <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{scope}[x=1.35cm,y=1.35cm]
    \def\thA{24}
    \def\thB{38}
    \def\ri{1.45}

    \draw[->] (-0.2,0) -- (2.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0,-0.2) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};

    \draw[dashed] (0,0) -- ({1.8*cos(\thA)},{1.8*sin(\thA)});
    \draw[dashed] (0,0) -- ({1.8*cos(\thB)},{1.8*sin(\thB)});

    \fill[blue!14]
      (0,0) -- ({\ri*cos(\thA)},{\ri*sin(\thA)})
      arc[start angle=\thA,end angle=\thB,radius=\ri]
      -- cycle;

    \draw[thick] (0,0) -- ({\ri*cos(\thA)},{\ri*sin(\thA)});
    \draw[thick] (0,0) -- ({\ri*cos(\thB)},{\ri*sin(\thB)});
    \draw[thick] ({\ri*cos(\thA)},{\ri*sin(\thA)}) arc[start angle=\thA,end angle=\thB,radius=\ri];

    \draw[<->] ({0.78*cos(\thA)},{0.78*sin(\thA)}) -- ({0.78*cos(\thB)},{0.78*sin(\thB)});
    \node[right] at (0.66,0.43) {$\Delta\theta$};
    \node[above] at ({0.52*\ri*cos((\thA+\thB)/2)},{0.52*\ri*sin((\thA+\thB)/2)}) {$r_i$};
    \node[above] at ({\ri*cos((\thA+\thB)/2)},{\ri*sin((\thA+\thB)/2)}) {$\Delta A_i$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
      ]]></latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>We must compute the wedge area <m>\Delta A_i</m>.
  Since <m>\Delta \theta</m> is tiny, the small curve segments
  are very close to straight lines, and <m>\Delta A_i</m> is a very thin triangle.
  Neglecting the small piece with radius larger that <m>r_i</m>,
  the slice <m>\Delta A_i</m> is approximately an isosceles triangle with height <m>r_i</m>
  and base <m>r_i\Delta\theta</m>.<fn>
  On a circle of radius <m>r</m>, and arc of <m>\theta</m> radians has length <m>r\theta</m>:
  this is the definition of radian measure.
  </fn>
  Thus:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\Delta A_i\
\approx\
\tfrac12\text{(base)}{\times}\text{(height)}
\ \approx\
\tfrac12(r_i\Delta\theta)r_i
\ =\
\tfrac12 r_i^2\Delta\theta
\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>The total area is the sum of these pieces, which is clearly a Riemann sum for an integral:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
A \ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n \Delta A_i
\ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n \tfrac12 r_i^2\Delta\theta
\ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n \tfrac12 f(\theta_i)^2\Delta\theta
\ =\ \int_a^b \tfrac12 f(\theta)^2\,d\theta\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>That is, the area inside a polar graph <m>r=f(\theta)</m> is given by an integral formula, but a different integral
  from the area under a rectangular graph <m>y=f(x)</m>.</p>

  <p><alert>Arclength in polar coordinates.</alert>
  Finally, we compute the length of the curve <m>r=f(\theta)</m> for <m>\theta\in[a,b]</m>.
  The length <m>L</m> is a sum of <m>n</m> increments <m>\Delta L_i</m>:</p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-10-4-delta-l-placeholder">
    <caption>Placeholder for 10-4-Delta-L.</caption>
    <image width="55%">
      <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 10-4-Delta-L.png.</shortdescription>
      <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{scope}[x=1.35cm,y=1.35cm]
    \def\thA{22}
    \def\thB{36}
    \def\ri{1.25}
    \def\dr{0.23}
    \pgfmathsetmacro\xA{\ri*cos(\thA)}
    \pgfmathsetmacro\yA{\ri*sin(\thA)}
    \pgfmathsetmacro\xB{(\ri+\dr)*cos(\thB)}
    \pgfmathsetmacro\yB{(\ri+\dr)*sin(\thB)}

    \draw[->] (-0.2,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0,-0.2) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};

    \draw[thick,domain=12:48,samples=140,smooth]
      plot ({(1.05+0.012*\x)*cos(\x)},{(1.05+0.012*\x)*sin(\x)});

    \draw[dashed] (0,0) -- ({1.8*cos(\thA)},{1.8*sin(\thA)});
    \draw[dashed] (0,0) -- ({1.8*cos(\thB)},{1.8*sin(\thB)});

    \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (\xA,\yA) -- (\xB,\yB);
    \draw[orange!80!black,thick] (\xA,\yA) -- ({(\ri+\dr)*cos(\thA)},{(\ri+\dr)*sin(\thA)});
    \draw[orange!80!black,thick] ({(\ri+\dr)*cos(\thA)},{(\ri+\dr)*sin(\thA)})
      arc[start angle=\thA,end angle=\thB,radius={\ri+\dr}];

    \fill (\xA,\yA) circle (1.1pt);
    \fill (\xB,\yB) circle (1.1pt);
    \node[right] at (\xA,\yA) {$P_i$};
    \node[right] at (\xB,\yB) {$P_{i+1}$};
    \node[above] at ({0.5*(\xA+\xB)},{0.5*(\yA+\yB)}) {$\Delta L_i$};
    \node[right] at ({(\ri+0.5*\dr)*cos(\thA)},{(\ri+0.5*\dr)*sin(\thA)}) {$\Delta r$};
    \node[above] at ({(\ri+\dr)*cos((\thA+\thB)/2)},{(\ri+\dr)*sin((\thA+\thB)/2)}) {$r_i\Delta\theta$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
      ]]></latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>Each increment <m>\Delta L_i</m> is approximately a straight line segment.
  Next to it is the radial segment <m>\Delta r</m> and
  the tiny circular arc with length <m>r_i\,\Delta\theta</m>,
  which is also approximately a straight line.
  We get an approximate right triangle with hypotentuse <m>\Delta L_i</m>
  and legs <m>r_i\,\Delta\theta</m> and <m>\Delta r</m>, so the Pythagorean Theorem gives:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\Delta L_i \
\approx\ \sqrt{(r_i\Delta \theta)^2+(\Delta r)^2}
\ =\
\sqrt{\tfrac {(r_i\Delta \theta)^2+(\Delta r)^2} {(\Delta \theta)^2}}\,\Delta\theta
\ =\
\sqrt{r_i^2 + (\tfrac{\Delta r}{\Delta \theta})^2}\,\Delta\theta\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>Therefore the total arclength is:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L \ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n \Delta L_i
\ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n \sqrt{ r_i^2 + (\tfrac{\Delta r}{\Delta\theta})^2 }\,\Delta\theta
\ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n \sqrt{ f(\theta_i)^2 + (\tfrac{\Delta f(\theta_i)}{\Delta\theta})^2 }\,\Delta\theta
\ =\ \int_a^b \sqrt{f(\theta)^2 + f'(\theta)^2}\,d\theta\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>We could also deduce this from our previous parametric arclength formula (<m>\S10.3</m>) by applying
  it to <m>(x(t),y(t))=(f(t)\cos(t), f(t)\sin(t))</m>.</p>

  <p><alert>Example: Area of intersections.</alert> Consider the polar curve <m>r = f(\theta)=1-\cos(\theta)</m>.
  We picture the abstract function <m>f</m> by its rectangular graph in <m>\theta r</m> parameter space
  (end <m>\S10.3</m>):</p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-10-4-cardioid-rect-placeholder">
    <caption>Placeholder for 10-4-Cardioid-Rect.</caption>
    <image width="50%">
      <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 10-4-Cardioid-Rect.png.</shortdescription>
      <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{scope}[x=1.0cm,y=1.0cm]
    \draw[->] (-0.2,0) -- (6.65,0) node[right] {$\theta$};
    \draw[->] (0,-0.2) -- (0,2.35) node[above] {$r$};

    \draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=0:6.28318,samples=260,smooth]
      plot (\x,{1-cos(\x r)});
    \draw[thick,red!70!black] (0.0,0.5) -- (6.28318,0.5);
    \draw[densely dotted] (1.0472,0) -- (1.0472,0.5);
    \draw[densely dotted] (5.2360,0) -- (5.2360,0.5);

    \node[below] at (1.0472,0) {$\tfrac\pi3$};
    \node[below] at (5.2360,0) {$\tfrac{5\pi}3$};
    \node[right] at (4.8,1.45) {$r=1-\cos\theta$};
    \node[right,red!70!black] at (4.7,0.6) {$r=\tfrac12$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
      ]]></latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>The polar graph is a cardioid (heart-shape), which we draw along with the circle <m>r=\frac12</m>.</p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-10-4-cardioid-placeholder">
    <caption>Placeholder for 10-4-Cardioid.</caption>
    <image width="55%">
      <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 10-4-Cardioid.png.</shortdescription>
      <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{scope}[x=1.45cm,y=1.45cm]
    \draw[->] (-2.1,0) -- (1.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0,-1.8) -- (0,1.8) node[above] {$y$};

    \fill[blue!10,domain=1.0472:5.2360,samples=260,smooth]
      (0,0)
      -- plot ({(1-cos(\x r))*cos(\x r)},{(1-cos(\x r))*sin(\x r)})
      -- cycle;

    \fill[white,domain=1.0472:5.2360,samples=220,smooth]
      (0,0)
      -- plot ({0.5*cos(\x r)},{0.5*sin(\x r)})
      -- cycle;

    \draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=0:6.28318,samples=420,smooth]
      plot ({(1-cos(\x r))*cos(\x r)},{(1-cos(\x r))*sin(\x r)});
    \draw[thick,red!70!black] (0,0) circle (0.5);

    \draw[densely dotted] (0,0) -- ({0.5*cos(60)},{0.5*sin(60)});
    \draw[densely dotted] (0,0) -- ({0.5*cos(300)},{0.5*sin(300)});
    \node[right] at (-1.45,1.2) {$r=1-\cos\theta$};
    \node[right,red!70!black] at (0.53,0.1) {$r=\tfrac12$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
      ]]></latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p><em>prob:</em> Find the area of the crescent-shaped region
  inside the cardioid &amp; outside the circle.</p>

  <p>We must first determine the intersection points of the two curves, where:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
r = 1-\cos(\theta) = \tfrac12
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\cos(\theta)=\tfrac12
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\theta= \pm\tfrac\pi3 + 2n\pi\,,
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>where <m>n</m> is any integer. Since the whole cardioid is traced by <m>\theta\in[0,2\pi]</m>,
  we can take all intersection points in this range: <m>\theta=\frac\pi3</m> and <m>\theta = -\frac{\pi}3 + 2\pi = \frac{5\pi}{3}</m>.
  Now we take the area inside the cardioid <m>r=f(\theta)=1-\cos(\theta)</m>,
  minus the area inside the circle <m>r=g(\theta)=\frac12</m>:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
A
\ =\ \int_a^b \tfrac12 f(\theta)^2 -\tfrac12 g(\theta)^2\ d\theta
\ =\ \int_{\pi/3}^{5\pi/3} \tfrac12(1{-}\cos(\theta))^2 - \tfrac12(\tfrac12)^2\ d\theta
\ =\ \left[\vphantom{\sum}\tfrac58\theta- \sin(\theta) + \tfrac18\sin(2\theta)\,\right]_{\theta=\pi/3}^{\theta=5\pi/3}
\ =\ \tfrac78\sqrt 3 +\tfrac{5\pi}6
\ \approx\ 4.1\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>To do the integral, expand <m>(1-\cos(\theta))^2</m> and use
  <m>\cos^2(\theta) = \frac12 + \frac12\cos(2\theta)</m> (see <m>\S7.2</m>).</p>

  <p><em>prob:</em> Find the highest point of the cardioid: <m>y(t) = (1-\cos(t))\sin(t) = \text{max}</m>. From <m>\S3.1</m>, we need to solve
  <m>y'(t) =0</m>. Using the Product Rule and <m>\sin^2=1-\cos^2</m> we get:
  <m>y'(t)=\sin(t)\sin(t) + (1-\cos(t))\cos(t) = -2\cos^2(t)+\cos(t)+1=0</m>, and the Quadratic formula gives
  <m>\cos(t) = -\frac12</m>, <m>t=\pm \frac{2\pi}{3}</m>. The maximum is <m>y(\frac{2\pi}{3}) = \frac{3\sqrt 3}{4}</m>.</p>

  <p><alert>Review example: Exponential spiral.</alert> Consider a snail-shell spiral curve which
  doubles in radius with each turn:</p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-10-4-double-spiral-placeholder">
    <caption>Placeholder for 10-4-Double-Spiral.</caption>
    <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 10-4-Double-Spiral.png.</shortdescription>
      <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{scope}[x=1.15cm,y=1.15cm]
    \def\b{0.1103178001}
    \draw[->] (-2.6,0) -- (2.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0,-2.3) -- (0,2.3) node[above] {$y$};

    \draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=-18.85:12.566,samples=500,smooth]
      plot ({exp(\b*\x)*cos(\x r)},{exp(\b*\x)*sin(\x r)});

    \draw[dashed] (1,0) circle (0.001);
    \node[above left] at (1.25,0.9) {$r=e^{b\theta},\ b=\frac{\ln 2}{2\pi}$};
    \node[below] at (1,0) {$(1,0)$};
    \draw[->,orange!80!black,thick] (1.6,0.2) arc[start angle=10,end angle=210,x radius=0.8,y radius=0.55];
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
      ]]></latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>This is the polar graph <m>r = f(\theta)=ca^\theta=ce^{b\theta}</m>
  of a general exponential function (<m>\S6.4</m>).
  Assuming <m>f(0)=1</m>, <m>f(2\pi)=2</m>, allows us to solve
  for <m>c=1</m> and <m>a= 2^{1/2\pi} =e^{\ln(2)/2\pi}</m> to get:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
r\,=\,2^{\,\theta/2\pi} \,=\, e^{b\theta}
\quad\text{for}\quad b=\tfrac{\ln(2)}{2\pi}\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>What is the length of this curve, from the point <m>(r,\theta)=(1,0)</m> all the way to the center,
  that is, for <m>\theta\in(-\infty,0]</m>?
  We have <m>f'(\theta)  \,=\, (e^{b\theta})' = be^{b\theta}</m>, so the arclength formula gives:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L \ = \int_{-\infty}^0\!\!\!\! \sqrt{f(\theta)^2 + f'(\theta)^2}\,d\theta
\ = \int_{-\infty}^0\!\!\!\! \sqrt{1{+}b^2}\, e^{b\theta}\,d\theta
\ = \left[ \tfrac1b\sqrt{1{+}b^2}\, e^{b\theta}\right]_{\theta=-\infty}^{\theta=0}
\ = \tfrac1b\sqrt{1{+}b^2} \, -\, \lim_{N\to\infty} \tfrac1b\sqrt{1{+}b^2}\, e^{-N}
\ = \sqrt{1{+}\tfrac{4\pi^2}{\ln^2(2)}} \approx 9.12\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>Or we could use geometry to show that these infinitely many turns have finite length.
  Let <m>L_1</m> be the length of the first turn <m>\theta\in[-2\pi,0]</m>, and <m>L_2</m> the length of the second turn, etc.
  The exponential spiral is <em>scale invariant</em>:
  each turn inward is the <m>\frac12</m> dilation of the previous turn,
  with half the length, so the total is a geomteric series
  <m>\sum_{n=1}^\infty cr^{n-1} = \frac c{1-r}</m> (<m>\S11.2</m>):</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L \,=\, L_1 +L_2+L_3+L_4+\cdots
\,=\,  L_1+\frac12 L_1 +\frac1{2^2}L_1+\frac1{2^3}L_1+\cdots
\,=\, \frac{L_1}{(1-\frac12)} \,=\, 2L_1.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p><alert>Harmonic Spiral.</alert> From the above, we may say that the inward spiral <m>r=1/2^{\theta}</m>
  has finite arclength as <m>\theta\to\infty</m> because the geometric series
  <m>\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac1{2^n}</m> is convergent.
  Let us instead model an inward spiral on the divergent harmonic series <m>\sum\frac1n=\infty</m>,
  namely <m>r=\frac1{\theta}</m> for <m>\theta\geq 1</m>. Then this should have infinite arclength:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L \ =\ \int_1^{\infty} \sqrt{(\frac{1}{\theta})^2+(-\frac{1}{\theta^2})^2}\,d\theta
\ =\ \int_1^{\infty} \frac{1}{\theta}\sqrt{1+\frac{1}{\theta^2}}\,d\theta
\ =\ \int_1^{\infty} \frac{\sqrt{1+\theta^2}}{\theta^2}\,d\theta.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>Since the integrand (in the second integral) is clearly positive and decreasing, the Integral Test  (<m>\S11.3</m>)
  tells us that this diverges whenever the corresponding series diverges, namely:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\sum_{n=1}^\infty  \frac{1}{n}\sqrt{1+\frac{1}{n^2}}
\  \approx\  \sum_{n=1}^\infty  \frac{1}{n} \ = \ \infty\  \text{(divergent)}.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>This can be justified by the Direct Comparison Test (<m>\S11.4</m>), since
  <m>\tfrac{1}{n}\sqrt{1{+}\tfrac{1}{n^2}}>\tfrac{1}{n}</m>; or
  the Limit Comparison Test: the ratio <m>\frac{a_n}{b_n}=\sqrt{1{+}\tfrac{1}{n^2}}\to 1</m>, so they have the same divergence.</p>

  <p>Alternatively, we can directly integrate,
  switching the variable to <m>\int\! \frac{\sqrt{1+x^2}}{x^2}\,dx</m>.
  Since we have <m>\sqrt{1{+}x^2}</m> (<m>\S7.3</m>), we try the trig substitution
  <m>x = \tan(t)</m>, <m>\sqrt{1{+}x^2}=\sec(t)</m>, <m>dx = \sec^2(t)\,dt</m>:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\int\! \frac{\sqrt{1+x^2}}{x^2}\,dx
\ =\ \int\! \frac{\sec(t)}{\tan^2(t)}\sec^2(t)\,dt
\ = \int\! \frac{1}{\sin^2(t)\cos(t)}\,dt
\ = \int\! \frac{1}{\sin^2(t)\cos^2(t)}\cos(t)\,dt.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>As in <m>\S7.2</m>, we do the substitution <m>u=\sin(t)</m>, <m>1{-}u^2 = \cos^2(t)</m>, <m>du = \cos(t)\,dt</m>:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\int\! \frac{1}{\sin^2(t)\cos^2(t)}\cos(t)\,dt
\ = \int\! \frac{1}{u^2(1{-}u^2)}\ du
\ = \int\! \frac{A}{u^2}+\frac{B}{u}+\frac{C}{1{+}u}+\frac{D}{1{-}u}\ du.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>Here the result is a rational function, expanded by partial fracitions (<m>\S7.4</m>).
  Then:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\frac{1}{u^2(1{-}u^2)}
\ = \frac{1{-}u^2}{u^2(1{-}u^2)} + \frac{u^2}{u^2(1{-}u^2)}
\ = \frac{1}{u^2} + \frac{1}{(1{-}u)(1{+}u)}
\ = \frac{1}{u^2} + \frac{C}{1{+}u}+\frac{D}{1{-}u}.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>We can find the remaining coefficients by clearing denominators to get</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
1= C(1{-}u)+D(1{+}u).
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>Substituting <m>u = 1</m> gives <m>D=\frac12</m>, and <m>u=-1</m> gives <m>C=\frac12</m>.
  The integral becomes:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\int \frac{1}{u^2}+\frac{\frac12}{1{+}u}+\frac{\frac12}{1{-}u}\ du
\ = -\frac{1}{u} + \tfrac12 \ln(1{+}u) - \tfrac12 \ln(1{-}u)
\ = -\frac{1}{u} + \frac12 \ln\!\left(\frac{1{+}u}{1{-}u}\right).
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>Now we need to restore the original variable <m>x=\tan(t)</m> from <m>u=\sin(t)</m>.
  The standard triangle for <m>x=\tan(t)</m> implies <m>u=\sin(t) = \frac{x}{\sqrt{1+x^2}}</m>.
  After simplification, the final answer is:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\int\! \frac {\sqrt{1{+}x^2}} {x^2}\,dx
\ =\ -\frac{\sqrt{1{+}x^2}}x
+ \frac12\ln\!\left(\!\frac {\sqrt{1{+}x^2}+x} {\sqrt{1{+}x^2}-x} \!\right).
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>Therefore the total arclength is:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L \ =\ \int_1^\infty\! \frac {\sqrt{1{+}x^2}} {x^2}\,dx
\ =\ \lim_{x\to\infty} \tfrac12 \ln\!\left(\!\frac {\sqrt{1{+}x^2}+x} {\sqrt{1{+}x^2}-x} \!\right) + K
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>for a constant <m>K</m>. In the fraction <m>\frac {\sqrt{1{+}x^2}+x} {\sqrt{1{+}x^2}-x}</m>,
  we <em>cannot</em> use L'Hopital's Rule (<m>\S6.8</m>), since the numerator clearly approaches <m>\infty</m>,
  but the denominator does not. Substitute <m>x=1/z</m>:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\lim_{x\to\infty} \sqrt{1{+}x^2}-x \ =\ \lim_{z\to0^+}\! \sqrt{1{+}\frac1{z^2}}-\frac 1z
\ =\ \lim_{z\to0^+}\! \frac{\sqrt{z^2{+}1}-1}z\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>This is a <m>\frac 00</m> limit, so we <em>can</em> apply L'Hopital to get:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\lim_{x\to\infty} \sqrt{1{+}x^2}-x
\ =\ \lim_{z\to0^+}\! \frac{(\sqrt{z^2{+}1}-1)'}{(z)'}
\ =\ \lim\limits_{z\to0^+}\! \frac {\frac z{\sqrt{z^2+1}}} {1} \ =\ 0^+.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>After all that, we obtain the expected arclength:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L \ =\ \tfrac12 \ln\!\left(\!\frac {\infty} {\ \;0^+} \!\right) + K
\ =\ \tfrac12 \ln(\infty) + K \ =\ \infty\,.
  ]]></md></p>

</section>
